In this lesson we'll look at working with formulas that have several variables. Lots of real-world formulas use more than one letter, like the area of a rectangle, which is length times width, or the perimeter of a triangle, which adds up three sides. First up, substituting into a multi-variable formula. The idea is simple: you swap letters for numbers, then calculate. Change the numbers, get a different answer, but the formula itself stays exactly the same. Next, rearranging or transposing. Sometimes you want a different letter to be the star of the show, standing alone on one side. That letter is called the subject of the formula. To swap who the subject is, just do the same thing to both sides, exactly like you'd do when solving an equation. So you might subtract, divide, or multiply your way around until the variable you want is on its own. Then there's systematic variation, which is a fancy way of saying, change one variable in a regular pattern, like adding one each time, while the others stay fixed. Watch what happens to the result. You'll often spot a neat pattern, like the answer growing by a steady amount each step, or doubling when you double an input. Get ready to describe those patterns in words too. Now let's see it in action.